[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Management and Clear Invocations in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing extensive unit test suites in Java using Mockito, memory management becomes a critical consideration. Mockito retains invocation history and stubbing data for every mock object in memory to facilitate verification. In long-running test suites or tests processing large datasets, this can lead to memory bloat and potential OutOfMemoryError issues."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Mockito's Internal State Retention"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every time a method is called on a mock, Mockito records the invocation details, arguments, and timestamps. If you reuse mock instances across multiple test methods without resetting them, the invocation history accumulates. This is particularly problematic in test classes annotated with @SpringBootTest or when using static mocks."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Using clearInvocations for Resetting State"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mockito provides the Mockito.clearInvocations() method to clear the invocation history of a mock without removing its stubbings. This allows you to free up memory and reset interaction counts mid-test without redefining your when(...) clauses."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"List<String> mockList = mock(List.class);
mockList.add("One");

// Verify interaction
verify(mockList).add("One");

// Clear invocations to free memory and reset interaction count
clearInvocations(mockList);

// Interaction count is now zero
verifyNoMoreInteractions(mockList);"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Difference Between clearInvocations and reset"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"clearInvocations(): Clears the invocation history but retains all stubbing definitions. Use this when you want to keep your mock behavior intact but forget past interactions."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"reset(): Resets the entire mock, clearing both invocation history and all stubbing definitions. Use this with caution, as Mockito documentation discourages frequent use of reset() in favor of creating fresh mocks."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Memory Management"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer fresh mock instances per test method instead of reusing mutable mocks across a large test suite."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Mockito.clearInvocations() in teardown methods or between heavy logical assertions in long multi-step integration tests."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always close static mocks using try-with-resources blocks when using Mockito.mockStatic() to prevent memory leaks and thread pollution."}]
